# Boltrig process environment (SRS S11). Copy to .env and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Process wiring (where the DB is, which secret store, the egress proxy) lives
# here; per-tenant POLICY lives in the fleet manifest (manifest.yaml). The same
# image runs every tenant: only this file and the manifest differ (P7).
#
# NEVER commit a real .env. Secrets belong in your secret store, not in git.
# The values referenced here are read by boltrig/config/settings.py and by the
# credential resolver (boltrig/kernel/credentials.py, EnvSecretStore).

# --- datastores -------------------------------------------------------------
# asyncpg driver for the kernel; host "postgres" is the compose service name.
# M9 (SEC-69): the password segment MUST match POSTGRES_PASSWORD below. There is
# no baked default any more - replace CHANGE_ME here and set POSTGRES_PASSWORD.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://boltrig:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/boltrig
# The test suite's Postgres leg. UNSET, ~156 tests silently skip - the RLS
# fence-drift guard, migration parity, tenancy and store parity among them - and
# a Postgres-only defect can sit behind a fully green local run (one did).
# Point it at a THROWAWAY database, NEVER the one above: these tests write.
#   BOLTRIG_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://boltrig:CHANGE_ME@postgres:5432/boltrig_test
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0
# External production Redis holds raw bounded run events: use authenticated
# `rediss://`, private networking and encrypted persistence/backups. /readyz
# verifies the Stream + transaction command capabilities, not only PING.
# Isolate relay keys when multiple Boltrig deployments intentionally share one
# Redis database. Every API/worker replica in one deployment must use the same
# value (letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen; default: default).
# BOLTRIG_EVENT_RELAY_NAMESPACE=production-a
# Optional real-service acceptance leg. Use a throwaway Redis database.
# BOLTRIG_TEST_REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/15

# Postgres bootstrap (consumed by the postgres service in docker-compose.yml).
# M9 (SEC-69): REQUIRED. docker-compose refuses to start unless POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# is set - there is deliberately NO default in the committed compose. Generate a
# strong value and keep it out of git:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
POSTGRES_USER=boltrig
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=boltrig

# --- audit (tamper-evident hash chain, SEC-16/K-19) -------------------------
# Per-deployment HMAC key for the audit chain. Generate a long random value:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
# LEFT BLANK DELIBERATELY ([2026] VJS-CC-BOLTRIG-AUDIT-KEY-PROVISIONING-001 O1).
# A placeholder here is worse than nothing: genesis.sh only mints a secret for an
# EMPTY value, so a shipped placeholder was copied verbatim into real .env files
# and the audit chain ran keyed by a public constant in this repository. Blank
# means provisioning fills it and boot fails closed if nothing did.
BOLTRIG_AUDIT_HMAC_KEY=

# --- enrolled desktop device lease signing (SEC-WRK-07) ---------------------
# One deployment-stable Ed25519 private seed, encoded as unpadded base64url.
# The public verifier and its SHA-256 key id are returned during enrollment; the
# seed never leaves the kernel. Blank fails device enrollment/lease routes closed
# with typed device_leases_unavailable while leaving the rest of Boltrig live.
# Generate once, store it in the deployment secret store, and retain the old
# verifier until every enrolled device has re-enrolled after a deliberate rotate:
#   python -c "import base64,secrets; print(base64.urlsafe_b64encode(secrets.token_bytes(32)).rstrip(b'=').decode())"
BOLTRIG_DEVICE_LEASE_SIGNING_KEY=

# --- secret store (SEC-04: the DB holds references only, never plaintext) ----
# One of: env | vault | kms | docker. "env" reads credential material from the
# per-adapter env vars below; each value is a JSON object.
SECRET_STORE=env

# --- credential sealing (SEC-04: no plaintext at rest in credential_refs) -----
# Passphrase that seals every credential_refs row at the store seam (envelope
# encryption; see boltrig/store/sealing.py). Unset falls back to an in-source
# dev key for offline dev/tests ONLY - a missing/default key is FATAL under a
# production signal. Generate a long random value:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
#BOLTRIG_SEAL_KEY=
# Optional previous passphrase, honoured for DECRYPT ONLY during rotation.
#BOLTRIG_SEAL_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# --- identity / OIDC (SEC-01/02; production MUST verify tokens) --------------
# When the OIDC trio is set the kernel verifies real bearer tokens
# (boltrig/identity/auth.py OidcVerifier). With no OIDC config the kernel refuses
# all requests (fail-closed) UNLESS BOLTRIG_DEV_AUTH=1, which enables the
# header-trusting dev resolver. NEVER set BOLTRIG_DEV_AUTH=1 in production.
OIDC_ISSUER=https://login.example.com/
OIDC_AUDIENCE=boltrig
OIDC_JWKS_URI=https://login.example.com/.well-known/jwks.json
# BOLTRIG_DEV_AUTH=1   # local dev only: trust x-boltrig-* headers (no verification)

# --- Cloudflare Access (zero-trust edge IdP; takes precedence over OIDC) ------
# When the team domain + AUD are set, the kernel verifies the per-request
# Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion against CF's JWKS and derives the principal from the
# authenticated email. Login / MFA / SSO happen at the Cloudflare edge; the
# kernel trusts only the verified assertion. CF_ACCESS_ROLE_MAP maps each allowed
# email to a platform role; an authenticated-but-unmapped email is denied
# (CF_ACCESS_DEFAULT_ROLE=none = fail-closed). Put app.boltrig.io behind a CF
# Access application, then set:
# CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=https://<team>.cloudflareaccess.com
# CF_ACCESS_AUD=<the Access application AUD tag>
# CF_ACCESS_ROLE_MAP={"you@example.com":"org-admin"}
# CF_ACCESS_DEFAULT_ROLE=none
# CF_ACCESS_TENANT=default

# The service-principal the agents act as for non-delegated calls (US-IAM-03).
AGENT_SP_ID=boltrig-agent

# --- notifications / HITL channel -------------------------------------------
# Configure a tenant-owned delivery route through the governed UI. No webhook
# or provider credential is bundled in the public environment template.
TEAMS_WEBHOOK=

# --- inference back ends -----------------------------------------------------
# Model provider keys do not belong in this file. Configure Bifrost's provider
# keys in its own admin surface or secret store, then select the advertised
# model in Settings. Boltrig never ships a provider key or a personal default.

# --- network posture (US-DEP-04, SEC) ---------------------------------------
# Corporate egress proxy (also pass as --build-arg to the Dockerfiles).
HTTP_PROXY=
HTTPS_PROXY=
NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1,postgres,redis,kernel,rivet-agentos,hatchet-engine,bifrost,local-model,langfuse
# Path to a custom CA bundle inside the container (mount it in if needed).
CA_BUNDLE=
# Set true to forbid all outbound network and force on-box inference (SEC-12).
AIR_GAPPED=false

# --- compose port overrides (optional) --------------------------------------
KERNEL_PORT=8000
WORKER_PORT=8082
HATCHET_GRPC_PORT=7077
HATCHET_API_PORT=8888
HATCHET_DASHBOARD_PORT=8889
LOCAL_MODEL_PORT=8001
# LOCAL_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct

# --- channel gateway + Worker realtime voice (profile-gated) ---------------
# Mint a show-once token in Worker's channel detail for the exact configured
# socket-channel ids. Prefer CHANNEL_GATEWAY_TOKEN_FILE with a read-only secret
# mount: the gateway hot-loads a changed token after authorization refusal.
# CHANNEL_GATEWAY_TOKEN is mutually exclusive and requires a restart to rotate.
CHANNEL_GATEWAY_TOKEN=
# Deployment-specific path inside the gateway container, for example
# /run/secrets/boltrig-channel-gateway-token. The base compose does not invent
# or mount a host secret path.
CHANNEL_GATEWAY_TOKEN_FILE=
# Development/test compatibility only. Production rejects static channel JSON;
# canonical desired state and credential references are authored in Worker.
CHANNEL_GATEWAY_CHANNELS=
CHANNEL_GATEWAY_RECONCILE_SECONDS=10
CHANNEL_GATEWAY_EGRESS_ALLOW=kernel,api.x.ai
# Bounded isolated provider sessions; a full pool refuses only the new call.
CHANNEL_GATEWAY_MAX_BROWSER_CALLS=8
# Public path returned to Worker. Candidate nginx routes this to the severed
# channel gateway and upgrades it to WebSocket.
BOLTRIG_CALL_WEBSOCKET_BASE=/voice/v1/calls

# --- M10 (SEC-70): scheduled off-box backup ---------------------------------
# Consumed by the profile-gated `backup` sidecar (docker compose --profile backup
# up -d), which loops scripts/backup.sh. Off-box copy + encryption are OPTIONAL:
# with BACKUP_REMOTE unset the sidecar still dumps locally and warns that the
# off-box leg was skipped (safe for dev). See docs/backup-restore.md.
# How often to run a backup, in seconds (default 24h).
BACKUP_INTERVAL=86400
# Additional seconds allowed after an interval before the last-success health
# marker is considered stale (default 1h, to cover a long dump/upload run).
BACKUP_HEALTH_GRACE=3600
# How many local dumps to keep (older ones are pruned).
BACKUP_KEEP=7
# Comma-separated recovery set. Durable Hatchet uses its own database; production
# must retain both or in-flight durable runs cannot be recovered with app state.
BACKUP_DATABASES=boltrig,hatchet
# Where the dumps land on the host (bind-mounted into the sidecar as /backups).
BACKUP_DIR=./backups
# Off-box destination as an rclone remote path (e.g. "s3:my-bucket/boltrig" or
# "b2:bucket/path"). UNSET => off-box copy is skipped with a warning (dev). SET =>
# a run fails loudly if the copy fails, so a broken remote cannot pass silently.
# BACKUP_REMOTE=s3:my-bucket/boltrig
# Directory holding your configured rclone.conf (mounted read-only). Only needed
# when BACKUP_REMOTE is set.
# RCLONE_CONFIG_DIR=./deploy/rclone
# Symmetric passphrase. The signed release path archives Hatchet signing state,
# Knowledge, libraries, and manifest data and therefore refuses to run without
# this value. Keep it in your secret store; without it a restore is impossible.
# BACKUP_PASSPHRASE=

# --- per-adapter credentials (refs resolved by EnvSecretStore as JSON) ------
# Each variable name is the credential ref named in manifest.yaml. The value is
# a JSON object of secret material handed to ONE adapter call inside the kernel
# and never returned to an agent or written to the audit log (SEC-05, K-20).
# MS Graph application credentials (ms-graph adapter).
GRAPH_APP={"tenant_id":"","client_id":"","client_secret":""}
# Jira OAuth / API token (jira adapter).
JIRA_OAUTH={"base_url":"https://your-org.atlassian.net","email":"","api_token":""}
# CRM SQL connection (crm-sql adapter).
CRM_SQL={"dsn":"postgresql://user:pass@crm-db:5432/crm"}

# --- Boltrig v2 selected stack ------------------------------------------------
# Rivet AgentOS runtime/sandbox. Unset URL -> rivet-worker degrades safely.
# RIVET_AGENTOS_URL=http://rivet-agentos:2468
# RIVET_AGENTOS_TOKEN=
# RIVET_AGENTOS_RUN_PATH=/runs
# BOLTRIG_RIVET_MCP_URL=http://kernel:8000/v1/mcp
# Browser Use CLI automation. Fleet and Hatchet each run a long-lived Chromium,
# so Compose keeps their auth/session/cache under different stack-owned roots.
# Never point either one at a developer profile or make the two values equal.
# BOLTRIG_FLEET_BROWSER_CLI_HOME=/var/lib/boltrig/browser-cli/fleet-worker
# BOLTRIG_HATCHET_BROWSER_CLI_HOME=/var/lib/boltrig/browser-cli/hatchet-worker
# A non-Compose, single-worker embedding may instead set the process-level
# BOLTRIG_BROWSER_CLI_HOME=/var/lib/boltrig/browser-cli
# BOLTRIG_BROWSER_CLI_BIN=/usr/local/bin/browser-use
# BOLTRIG_BROWSER_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=
# Browser Use cloud profile handoff is disabled by default. Set policy=stack to
# pass only these BOLTRIG_* values into the child process as Browser Use env.
# Never set personal BROWSER_USE_* variables in the service environment.
# BOLTRIG_BROWSER_CLOUD_POLICY=disabled
# BOLTRIG_BROWSER_CLOUD_API_KEY=
# BOLTRIG_BROWSER_CLOUD_PROFILE_ID=
# BOLTRIG_BROWSER_CLOUD_PROJECT_ID=
# BOLTRIG_BROWSER_CLOUD_TEAM_ID=
# Stack-owned Herdr/OpenCode state. Compose defaults these to named volumes, but
# production doctor expects explicit values in non-compose or checked env files.
# Do not point them at a developer user's ~/.config, ~/.local, or .opencode state.
# BOLTRIG_HERDR_HOME=/var/lib/boltrig/herdr
# BOLTRIG_OPENCODE_HOME=/var/lib/boltrig/opencode
# First-party Knowledge: canonical originals plus the bundled Cognee compiler.
# Compose persists both paths in stack-owned named volumes. Cognee remains a
# rebuildable projection; absent model configuration is reported as degraded and
# never prevents the canonical upload from committing.
# BOLTRIG_KNOWLEDGE_VAULT=/var/lib/boltrig/knowledge
# BOLTRIG_COGNEE_ROOT=/var/lib/boltrig/cognee
# LLM_PROVIDER=openai
# LLM_MODEL=
# LLM_ENDPOINT=http://bifrost:8080/v1
# LLM_API_KEY=
# EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=fastembed
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=
# Supermemory and Mem0 are disabled add-ons until their credential-backed
# connections are configured, then enabled through the governed provider action.
# SUPERMEMORY_API_KEY=
# MEM0_API_KEY=
# MEM0_ORG_ID=
# MEM0_PROJECT_ID=
# Langfuse observability sink.
# BOLTRIG_LANGFUSE_ENABLED=true
# LANGFUSE_HOST=http://langfuse:3000
# LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=
# LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=

# --- Round Six: conversation continuity + model gateway ---------------------
# Continuity composes prior turns into the prompt (gap 3.1). On by default; set
# to 0 to restore the prior single-message behaviour exactly.
BOLTRIG_CONTINUITY=1
# Model gateway (gap 3.2): when set, standard conversation traffic routes through
# the gateway, pinned to one model per conversation for cache warmth (SEC-47).
# Sensitive data is never routed here (residency). Unset => the seam is inert.
# To activate: `docker compose --profile gateway up -d` (starts the bifrost
# service), configure provider keys in the Bifrost UI at http://localhost:8081,
# then uncomment the line below. Bifrost is OpenAI-compatible inside compose on
# :8080; Pi appends /chat/completions, so the gateway URL includes /v1.
# BOLTRIG_MODEL_GATEWAY_URL=http://bifrost:8080/v1
BOLTRIG_MODEL_GATEWAY_TTL=900
# Optional live gateway health for /v1/platform/status. Disabled by default; when
# enabled, Boltrig derives http://bifrost:8080/health from the /v1 URL unless an
# explicit internal health URL/path is set. External hosts are not polled.
# BOLTRIG_MODEL_GATEWAY_HEALTH=0
# BOLTRIG_MODEL_GATEWAY_HEALTH_PATH=/health
# BOLTRIG_MODEL_GATEWAY_HEALTH_URL=
# BOLTRIG_MODEL_GATEWAY_HEALTH_TIMEOUT=0.75
# BIFROST_PORT=8081

# --- trusted read-only Codex ([2026] VJS-CC-VJS 2) ---------------------------
# Opt-in, DEV ONLY: run a trusted single-tenant read-only Codex runtime. Lawful
# only hard-walled from production - it needs BOLTRIG_DEV_AUTH=1 and refuses under
# any production signal or real ingress posture. The kernel image ships the pinned
# binary; cells need the exec tmpfs mount (see docker-compose.yml kernel tmpfs).
# All four are required together; any missing value leaves Codex OFF (a no-op).
# BOLTRIG_CODEX_TRUSTED=1
# BOLTRIG_CODEX_BINARY=/opt/boltrig/codex/codex
# BOLTRIG_CODEX_STACK_ROOT=/var/lib/boltrig/codex-cells
# BOLTRIG_CODEX_MODEL=glm-4.6
# BOLTRIG_MODEL_GATEWAY_KEY=   # kernel-only upstream key the proxy injects (dev bifrost is unauth)

# --- Deep readiness ---------------------------------------------------------
# /readyz always verifies control-plane registration and stack-tool posture. In
# production it also requires DATABASE_URL + REDIS_URL, the Redis-backed event
# relay (no in-memory fallback), and the exact Alembic head. Production
# additionally runs Herdr in the kernel and requires a fresh
# HMAC-authenticated Redis receipt from fleet-local OpenCode, Browser Use, and
# Chromium probes; the kernel never assumes those fleet-owned binaries share its
# container. Receipt authentication derives a purpose-separated key from the
# deployment's BOLTRIG_AUDIT_HMAC_KEY. Each readiness probe is bounded by this
# timeout (clamped to 0.05..10s).
BOLTRIG_READINESS_TIMEOUT=0.75
# Coalesce concurrent unauthenticated probes and cache only the redacted result
# briefly, preventing orchestrator/hostile polling from amplifying subprocesses.
# BOLTRIG_READINESS_CACHE_TTL=1
# Require password-reset delivery only when the embedding deployment injects
# both a reviewed notifier and a bounded, redacted readiness probe. The standard
# ASGI composition intentionally injects neither. Readiness proves adapter
# posture only, never a provider receipt or inbox delivery.
# BOLTRIG_REQUIRE_PASSWORD_RESET_DELIVERY=0
# Require live stack-tool evidence outside production too (default: posture-only
# compatibility in development). Fleet receipts expire and cannot survive a
# dead worker; interval and probe time are cross-bounded against TTL. All values
# are bounded.
# BOLTRIG_REQUIRE_STACK_TOOL_HEALTH=0
# BOLTRIG_STACK_TOOL_RECEIPT_TTL=30
# BOLTRIG_STACK_TOOL_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=10
# BOLTRIG_STACK_TOOL_PROBE_TIMEOUT=4
# Hatchet is probed only when explicitly enabled, durability is required, or a
# HATCHET_CLIENT_TOKEN is configured. A configured-but-unreachable engine makes
# /readyz fail closed instead of silently accepting the local executor.
# BOLTRIG_HATCHET_HEALTH=0
# BOLTRIG_REQUIRE_DURABLE=0
# REQUIRED whenever HATCHET_CLIENT_TOKEN is set against the bundled hatchet-lite:
# the engine serves PLAINTEXT gRPC on the compose network but the SDK defaults to
# TLS, so without this the client dies with an SSL WRONG_VERSION_NUMBER handshake
# error, /readyz reports hatchet probe_failed, and execution silently drops to the
# non-durable local executor. Both prod stacks ran that way unnoticed until
# 2026-07-25 (docs/findings/2026-07-25-prod-roll-0.3.1.md).
HATCHET_CLIENT_TLS_STRATEGY=none
# Mint against the stack's OWN engine; --config /config is required or the admin
# CLI cannot build a session store:
#   docker exec <stack>-hatchet-engine-1 /hatchet-admin --config /config \
#     token create --name <name> --tenant-id <tenant>
# Compose persists /config in the hatchet_config volume. Preserve and restore it
# with the Hatchet database; losing that volume regenerates signing keys, makes
# every existing token UNAUTHENTICATED, and requires a deliberate re-mint.
# HATCHET_CLIENT_TOKEN=

# --- Round Sixteen: edge/web hardening (WEB-*, RES-01) + prod guard (IAM-09) ----
# Host allowlist for the kernel app (TrustedHost). Default '*' (dev); a deployment
# SHOULD set its real hostnames.
# BOLTRIG_ALLOWED_HOSTS=api.example.com
# Browser CORS origin allowlist (never '*' with credentials). Default: same-origin.
# BOLTRIG_CORS_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com
# Request-body size cap in bytes (default 1 MiB).
# BOLTRIG_MAX_BODY_BYTES=1048576
# A production signal: with BOLTRIG_DEV_AUTH=1 set, the kernel REFUSES to start if
# any of ENV/BOLTRIG_ENV/APP_ENV is prod|production|staging or BOLTRIG_PRODUCTION=1.
# BOLTRIG_PRODUCTION=1
# Believe the edge's X-Forwarded-Prefix, so the session cookies are scoped to the
# console's mount instead of the whole host. Set this ONLY where a reverse proxy
# strips a prefix and sets that header itself (a <host>/boltrig mount); the header
# is client-settable, so it is ignored by default. Note this is a BOOLEAN, not the
# path: the mount stays derived, never configured.
# BOLTRIG_TRUST_FORWARDED_PREFIX=1

# --- Desktop hands add-on (DH-1, decision 0016) --------------------------------
# Opt-in: the governed desktop.* verbs and the /v1/hands executor pull surface are
# registered only when this is 1 AND the host executor (familiar-hands) is installed
# on the desktop host. Default OFF: no adapter, no verbs, no pull surface.
# BOLTRIG_DESKTOP_HANDS=1

# --- Addons (integration seams; see docs/addons.md) ---------------------------
# Which integrations this deployment activates, comma-separated. A boltrig
# shipping ALONE sets nothing and carries no integration vocabulary at all.
# Set this on a tenant provisioned by that product, and only there.
#
# Registration is not activation, and a name that is not registered RAISES: a
# typo must not quietly ship a boltrig with the integration missing, because that
# failure is silent (the turn completes and the agent apologises).
#
# With `opbox` active, the chat turn seals the caller's clamped session bearer
# for the opbox adapter (permission-parity passthrough) and the model is told how
# opbox record identifiers work. Without it, neither happens.
# BOLTRIG_ADDONS=opbox
#
# Override only if the consumed server is deployed under a different adapter id.
# BOLTRIG_OBO_ADAPTER_ID=opbox

# Reflection: the only automatic memory writer. When 1, a terminal work item is
# distilled into a `lesson` memory via memory.remember under a bounded grant.
# OFF by default because it retains derived content (memory.retention_days = 365).
# It was set on no deployment and documented nowhere, so boltrig's memory tables were
# empty on every box while the subsystem reported itself enabled.
BOLTRIG_REFLECT=

# DB-enforced tenant isolation (SEC-65). When 1, PostgresStore wraps its pool in
# _RlsPool so every store call binds app.tenant_id per transaction. OFF by default.
#
# THE FLAG ALONE DOES NOTHING, AND THE ORDER MATTERS. Activation is three steps:
#   1. set BOLTRIG_RLS=1 and restart, so the app binds the GUC on every call
#   2. THEN apply the overlay as the table owner: store.apply_rls() (boltrig/store/rls.sql)
#   3. connect the app as the least-privilege boltrig_app role (NOBYPASSRLS)
# Doing 2 before 1 takes the stack DOWN rather than making it safe: rls.sql uses FORCE
# ROW LEVEL SECURITY, so even the owner is subject, and a null app.tenant_id yields
# ZERO ROWS by design. That fail-closed behaviour is correct and is exactly why the
# sequence is not reorderable. Rollback is to drop the policies, not to unset the flag.
#
# Scope: the overlay fences tenant_id ONLY. Per-user isolation inside a tenant
# (memory owner_scope) is still application-level - see rbac.memory_owner_scopes.
#
# SAFE TO ENABLE as of 2026-07-30, and here is what it cost to make it so.
#
# The fence used to read the _current_tenant CONTEXTVAR while every store method
# takes the tenant as an ARGUMENT: 318 tenant-carrying coroutines against 51
# set_current_tenant sites, with no middleware. Turning this on in that state killed
# the kernel at boot ("new row violates row-level security policy for table
# model_endpoints"). The write case is the loud one; an unbound READ does not error,
# it returns ZERO ROWS silently, so data appears to vanish rather than fail.
#
# bind_tenant_on_store_methods (store/tenant_scope.py) now derives the GUC from the
# tenant the call was already given, so the two cannot drift. It is applied to BOTH
# holders of an _RlsPool - PostgresStore and PostgresKnowledgeRepository - and a test
# sweeps for a third.
#
# MEASURED ON THE BEELINK with a query carrying NO tenant predicate: a raw owner
# connection saw 4 rows, the app's own pool saw 3 bound to one tenant, 1 bound to the
# other, and 0 with nothing bound. The activation order below still holds.
BOLTRIG_RLS=
